### Core Concepts

A function is a relation between a set of inputs, called the domain, and a set of possible outputs, called the codomain. The function assigns to each element of the domain exactly one element of the codomain. In other words, for every input, there is a unique output. This is denoted by the notation $f: A \\to B$, where $A$ is the domain and $B$ is the codomain.

A function can be represented as a set of ordered pairs, where each ordered pair consists of an input from the domain and its corresponding output from the codomain. For example, the function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) = 2x + 1$ can be represented as the set of ordered pairs $\\{(x, 2x + 1) \\mid x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\}
